    "fields": "All 12 named DNS/RANS wall files contain the four columns y(m), T(K), q_w(W/m²), tau_w(Pa), with finite values and identical y coordinates between the two RANS files on each wall."
  },
  "analysis": "For each of the four walls and each observable, compare both complete model profiles to the same DNS over the entire common y support. Piecewise-linear interpolation using the union of source knots, with no endpoint extrapolation. Compute RMSE=sqrt(int[(Q_model-Q_DNS)^2]dy/(ymax-ymin)), exact element formula dy*(e0²+e0 e1+e1²)/3; relative L2 (%)=100*||Q_model-Q_DNS||₂/||Q_DNS||₂. Compute exact linear absolute-error integral with zero crossings for MAE and recompute after trimming 5% of common span at either end. This is a newly defined retrospective summary of deposited profiles, not an author-specified tolerance or uncertainty.",
  "findings": "Switching from scalable to two-scale lowers wall-shear RMSE on every wall. It lowers heat-flux RMSE on hot, cold and baffle-left walls. On baffle-right the shear RMSE falls from 0.002442 to 0.000855 Pa while heat flux worsens from 118.16 to 155.29 W/m². Baffle-right relative L2 errors therefore change from 69.39 to 24.28% for shear and 34.09 to 44.80% for heat flux. Both comparisons use the identical baffle-right y interval [0.45187, 1.8716] m, same runs and same DNS. The same opposing signs survive MAE and trimming 5% off each end. They are not a change of wall or station, nor merely a threshold crossing. These directions do not extend to the other three walls.",
  "caveat": "These are numerical distances to the shared DNS in a coupled buoyancy/thermal field. The archive contains means, with no per-profile sampling covariance or statistical inference in this analysis. The two-scale and scalable formulations change the coupled near-wall treatment; the pair does not isolate a single physical source of discrepancy.",
